## msil-ld on msil64-unknown-dotnet: the target gate, inputs that bind
## native code, and undefined C library names (ImplementationPlan.md 5.18.6
## PL1-PL3, 5.14.2's N6 and N2 rows; P-1a's texts T-P1 and T-P4):
## - an msil64 object links with no --target, with
##   --target=msil64-unknown-dotnet and with the short spelling
##   --target=msil64-dotnet (getMSILTriple normalizes it), to the same
##   image;
## - msil32-unknown-dotnet is T-P1:
##     target 'msil32-unknown-dotnet' is not supported yet: only msil64-unknown-dotnet is implemented
##   while the combinations that 5.18.1 PT5 refuses for good have
##   TargetRegistry's reason, as llc reports it, naming the triple as given:
##   an msil architecture without the OS dotnet (msil64-unknown-linux), and
##   the OS dotnet without an msil architecture (x86_64-unknown-dotnet, not
##   x86_64-unknown-dotnet-msil), and an msil architecture with an object
##   format other than MSIL (msil64-unknown-dotnet-elf, -coff: 'the
##   architecture 'msil64' produces only MSIL, not the '<format>' object
##   format'; until P-1b only the clang driver's case pinned it);
##   portable-guard.test has the environment reason;
## - host and portable objects never mix: an object whose record triple key
##   differs is refused as today (obj-triple.test's text), in either order
##   and against --target;
## - every native library is T-P4,
##     '<file>' is a native library, which msil64-unknown-dotnet cannot link: the portable target binds no native code
##   a shared object, a native DLL, a COFF import library, a static library
##   of COFF objects, a MinGW import library ('.dll.a') and an archive whose
##   only member is an ELF object, given by path, and that archive found by
##   -l too, also with -l before the first object and no --target (the -l
##   search waits for the codegen triple, so it takes the native candidate
##   that reading reports), and an archive of native members named before
##   the first object with no --target, where the object fixes the target
##   only after the archive is read: the rule waits for the codegen triple,
##   so the order of the arguments does not matter (section 6 P-1a
##   'Archives on dotnet'),
##   also when the program references the archive's native function;
## - a refused --target is reported before any input is judged: with a
##   native archive given by path, before or after the object, msil32 is
##   T-P1 and x86_64-unknown-dotnet and msil64-unknown-dotnet-gnu have
##   PT5's reason, never T-P4; with only an empty archive,
##   msil64-unknown-dotnet-gnu has PT5's reason, not 'no input files';
## - a native COFF or ELF object given on the command line is no library and
##   no error by itself: msil-ld skips it with the '--verbose' line
##     native object <f>: its definitions are machine code, which msil64-unknown-dotnet never links
##   (also named before the first object under --target; with no --target
##   such a leading native object is still skipped and the image is the
##   same, but its '--verbose' line is not pinned here), the program links
##   as without it, and a reference to one of its definitions is a
##   plain 'undefined symbol: <name>' with its '>>> referenced by' line, never
##   M22's 'defined only in native object code' error (section 12, P-1a,
##   5.18.6 PL2 and PL6 as built);
## - an archive that mixes CIL and native members links its CIL members and
##   skips the native ones ('--verbose' logs each skipped member), named
##   after the object or before it, to the same image;
## - an archive with no members (the portable sysroot's libm.a, PL2; brief
##   Q14 (a)) is not a native library: it contributes nothing, by path and
##   by -l, whether it is the bare '!<arch>' header or llvm-ar's archive
##   with an empty symbol table, and named before the first object too;
## - an undefined C library name gets the class line N6 after the first line
##   and before '>>> referenced by': '>>> a C library function; link the
##   managed C library c.dll (-lc)' without an input named c.dll, and
##   '>>> a C library function that the C library c.dll
##   (msil64-unknown-dotnet) does not provide' when one takes part (by path
##   or through -lc). malloc is a TargetLibraryInfo name and pthread_create a
##   name of lld/MSIL/CLibraryNames.def (llvm-libc's public names that TLI
##   lacks); a name in neither source keeps the plain report;
## - N2's portable line for an '__atomic_*' name, '>>> an atomic operation
##   (libatomic's interface), which msil64-unknown-dotnet does not provide
##   yet', and N1's and N3's lines unchanged, none with N6's line;
## - a weak undefined function keeps today's non-Windows rule (null, no
##   error);
## - an undefined data reference (glibc's stdout and environ) is a plain
##   undefined-symbol error with no class line and no native binding (PL2:
##   no data import on dotnet), and libc.so.6 is T-P4 for it; with a CIL
##   object that defines both, the image has no moduleref, no pinvokeimpl
##   and no '<msil>dataimport' helper. The same object built for
##   x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu-msil binds both from libc.so.6 through the
##   data-import helpers, which shows what the checks would catch;
## - linker options in an msil64 object are GNU-tokenized, as on ELF
##   triples (portable-linkrules D10): one string is one option, so
##   '-lmy lib' names the library 'my lib' (Windows tokenization would split
##   it into 'my' and 'lib'), and a pragma's '-lm' resolves to the
##   sysroot-style empty libm.a (brief Q14).
## Objects only: the portable triples have no LTO coverage before P-3
## (brief Q13 (a)).

# RUN: rm -rf %t && split-file %s %t && cd %t
# RUN: mkdir plain short long crt e1 e2 e3 e4 e5 mix-after mix-before
# RUN: llc -filetype=obj p.ll -o p.o
# RUN: llc -filetype=obj host.ll -o host.o
# RUN: llc -filetype=obj cstub.ll -o cstub.o
# RUN: llc -filetype=obj malloc.ll -o malloc.o
# RUN: llc -filetype=obj pthread.ll -o pthread.o
# RUN: llc -filetype=obj zork.ll -o zork.o
# RUN: llc -filetype=obj helpers.ll -o helpers.o
# RUN: llc -filetype=obj weak.ll -o weak.o

